.Sh DESCRIPTION
A reader or writer is a backend context owned by the caller and a handle
that points to it.
Each constructor fills
.Fa *ctx
and returns the handle, which holds
.Fa ctx
and a copy of the backend's vtable.
The context, and any buffer given to the constructor, must outlive the
handle and must not move while it is in use.
Nothing allocates except the growable writer, which grows its vector through
the allocator it was given.
.Pp
The backends are
.Dq fs ,
a file descriptor on Linux or a
.Vt HANDLE
on Windows,
.Dq mem ,
a caller-owned memory region, and
.Dq growable ,
a
.Vt t_vec .
.Ss Types
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_reader {
	t_any			reader_ctx;
	t_reader_vtable	vtable;	/* stream, discard, stream_all,
				   discard_all, read, read_all */
} t_reader;

typedef struct s_writer {
	t_any			writer_ctx;
	t_writer_vtable	vtable;	/* drain_all, flush */
} t_writer;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_fs {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* staging for stream and discard */
	t_fd		fd;
} t_reader_fs;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_memory {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* bytes not yet read */
} t_reader_ctx_memory;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_growable {
	t_vec		vec;	/* bytes not yet read */
} t_reader_ctx_growable;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_fs {
	t_buffer_with_capacity	buffer;	/* staged bytes */
	t_fd					fd;
} t_writer_fs;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_memory {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* room not yet written */
} t_writer_ctx_memory;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_growable {
	t_allocator	allocator;
	t_vec		vec;	/* bytes written */
} t_writer_ctx_growable;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_iovec
holds
.Fa mem
and
.Fa size
and is the element type of
.Fn xft_writev .
.Ss Readers
.Fn xft_reader_stream
moves up to
.Fa limit
bytes from
.Fa r
into
.Fa w
through
.Fn xft_writer_drain_all
and stores the count in
.Pf * Fa size .
It stops at
.Fa limit
or at the end of the input, so a count below
.Fa limit
means the input is exhausted.
.Fn xft_reader_discard
consumes up to
.Fa limit
bytes the same way and drops them.
.Fn xft_reader_read
copies into
.Fa dst
until it is full or the input ends and stores the count in
.Pf * Fa size .
.Pp
.Fn xft_reader_stream_all ,
.Fn xft_reader_discard_all
and
.Fn xft_reader_read_all
fail unless exactly
.Fa limit ,
or
.Fa dst Ns .size ,
bytes were transferred.
Bytes transferred before the input ended are still delivered and consumed.
.Pp
The fs reader reads at most
.Fa buffer Ns .size
bytes into its staging buffer per call and drains each chunk to the writer.
.Fn xft_reader_read
on an fs reader reads straight into
.Fa dst .
A read that returns no bytes is the end of the input.
On Linux interrupted calls are retried; on Windows a broken pipe is the end
of the input.
.Pp
The mem reader takes bytes from the front of
.Fa src
and advances
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer
past them.
The growable reader takes bytes from the front of
.Fa vec
and moves the remaining bytes down, shrinking
.Fa ctx Ns ->vec.buf.size .
.Ss Writers
.Fn xft_writer_drain_all
writes all of
.Fa data
to
.Fa w .
.Fn xft_writer_flush
pushes out anything the writer has staged.
.Pp
The fs writer stages bytes in
.Fa buffer ,
whose size becomes the staging capacity.
Data that fits in the free space is only copied there.
Otherwise the staged bytes and
.Fa data
are written together, with one
.Fn xft_writev
call on Linux and one
.Ql WriteFile
call per piece on Windows, repeated until everything is written, and the
staging buffer is left empty.
.Fn xft_writer_flush
writes the staged bytes with
.Fn xft_write
or
.Ql WriteFile
until none are left.
Nothing is flushed implicitly; staged bytes are lost unless
.Fn xft_writer_flush
is called.
.Pp
The mem writer copies into
.Fa dst
and advances
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer
past the bytes written, so the bytes written so far are
.Fa dst Ns .size
minus
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer.size .
The growable writer appends to
.Fa ctx Ns ->vec
with
.Fn xft_vec_extend .
For both,
.Fn xft_writer_flush
does nothing.
The caller still owns the vector and frees it with
.Fn xft_vec_destroy
and
.Fa ctx Ns ->allocator .
.Ss Standard handles
.Fn xft_get_stdin ,
.Fn xft_get_stdout
and
.Fn xft_get_stderr
return the handles of the standard streams: 0, 1 and 2 on Linux, and the
results of
.Ql GetStdHandle
on Windows.
.Ss File information
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_name
fills
.Pf * Fa fileinfo
for the file
.Fa name
without following a final symbolic link, so a link reports
.Dv F_T_SYML .
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_fd
does the same for an open handle.
On Linux both use
.Xr statx 2 .
On Windows a handle that is not a disk file, such as a pipe or a console,
only gets its
.Fa type
filled in.
Times are nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
.Vt t_file_info
is described in
.Xr xft_files 3 .
.Ss File mapping
.Fn xft_map_file_into_memory
maps the file
.Fa name
read-only and private and closes the descriptor before returning; the
mapping stays valid.
.Fn xft_unmap_file_from_memory
unmaps a buffer it returned.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The reader and writer functions return
.Dv OK
on success and
.Dv KO
on failure.
An fs handle fails when a read or write fails or a write makes no progress.
A mem writer fails, without copying anything, when
.Fa data
is larger than the room left.
A growable writer fails when the vector cannot grow.
Mem and growable readers never fail.
.Pp
The constructors return the handle and cannot fail.
.Pp
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_name
and
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_fd
return
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
if the file or handle cannot be queried.
.Pp
.Fn xft_map_file_into_memory
returns the mapped bytes and the file size, or a zeroed
.Vt t_buffer
if opening, querying or mapping the file fails.
An empty file cannot be mapped and also yields a zeroed buffer.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy standard input to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft/xft.h>

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	t_u8		inbuf[4096];
	t_u8		outbuf[4096];
	t_reader_fs	rctx;
	t_writer_fs	wctx;
	t_reader	r;
	t_writer	w;
	t_size		n;

	(void)sp;
	r = xft_fs_reader(&rctx, xft_get_stdin(),
			xft_fatptr(inbuf, sizeof(inbuf)));
	w = xft_fs_writer(&wctx, xft_get_stdout(),
			xft_fatptr(outbuf, sizeof(outbuf)));
	if (!xft_reader_stream(&r, &w, (t_size)-1, &n))
		xft_exit_process(1);
	xft_exit_process(!xft_writer_flush(&w));
}
.Ed
.Pp
Collect a reader's whole input in memory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_result	slurp(t_allocator a, t_reader *r, t_writer_ctx_growable *ctx)
{
	t_vec		vec;
	t_writer	w;
	t_size		n;

	if (!xft_new_vec(a, &vec, 4096, 1))
		return (KO);
	w = xft_growable_writer(ctx, a, vec);
	if (!xft_reader_stream(r, &w, (t_size)-1, &n))
		return (xft_vec_destroy(a, &ctx->vec), KO);
	return (OK);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_async 3 ,
